1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,640
Welcome, let's dive right into this new exploration.

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:08,640
Today we are analyzing an economics paper that is truly incredible for its methodological rigor.

3
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:11,359
Written by Alberto Alesina and Eliana La Ferrara,

4
00:00:11,359 --> 00:00:14,599
this study addresses a deeply sensitive topic,

5
00:00:14,599 --> 00:00:17,199
the death penalty and racial bias.

6
00:00:17,199 --> 00:00:21,120
And it does so in a unique way, using exclusively pure statistics,

7
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:24,240
leaving no room for personal opinions or speculation.

8
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:27,800
It is a fascinating work that helps us understand how the tools of economics

9
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:32,480
can bring to light hidden dynamics right at the heart of the justice system.

10
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:35,640
Let's start with a fact that is certainly thought-provoking,

11
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:41,920
according to Amnesty International, African Americans make up more than 40% of current inmates

12
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:44,280
on death row in the United States.

13
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:48,159
This is the factual premise from which the whole study begins.

14
00:00:48,159 --> 00:00:51,359
In practice, it tells us that minorities are represented in a way that is

15
00:00:51,359 --> 00:00:55,799
enormously disproportionate on death row compared to their actual weight

16
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:57,800
in the general population.

17
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:00,440
And here, a question naturally arises,

18
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:04,600
but is this simple raw data enough to prove a bias?

19
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:09,560
Well, in the economic-statistical field, jumping to conclusions by looking at a single aggregate number

20
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:11,040
is super risky.

21
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:13,439
This numerical disparity, however strong,

22
00:01:13,439 --> 00:01:18,480
is absolutely not sufficient to scientifically prove a bias in the system.

23
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:23,280
We must necessarily ask ourselves if there are other hidden factors driving these figures

24
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:25,280
before we can issue a verdict.

25
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:29,320
Here is how we will proceed today: one, the limitation of the data,

26
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:32,159
two, we will analyze judicial errors,

27
00:01:32,159 --> 00:01:34,359
three, the logic of the model,

28
00:01:34,359 --> 00:01:37,320
four, the results on a national scale,

29
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:39,400
five, the role of the South,

30
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:42,640
and finally, six, the conclusions of the study.

31
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:46,000
Let's start with the first point, the limitation of the data,

32
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,280
meaning the real problem with raw statistics.

33
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:52,640
But why is stopping at simple conviction rates a mistake?

34
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:55,120
The reason is that violent crime rates,

35
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:57,239
like many other social factors,

36
00:01:57,239 --> 00:01:59,719
vary across different demographic groups.

37
00:01:59,719 --> 00:02:04,519
Here is where what we call in statistics the omitted variables bias comes into play.

38
00:02:04,519 --> 00:02:06,159
To perform a rigorous test,

39
00:02:06,159 --> 00:02:09,360
a model must absolutely consider those hidden factors

40
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:11,680
that could be linked to ethnicity.

41
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:13,280
Let's use a practical example.

42
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:17,400
If a certain group happened to commit crimes with more aggravating circumstances,

43
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:20,680
such as multiple victims or a heavier criminal record,

44
00:02:20,719 --> 00:02:24,319
a simple average conviction rate would seem biased,

45
00:02:24,319 --> 00:02:28,080
even if the court were judging in a perfectly fair manner.

46
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:32,080
In short, we are missing too many puzzle pieces to judge solely by the totals.

47
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:34,159
Let's move on to the second point,

48
00:02:34,159 --> 00:02:36,439
analyzing judicial errors,

49
00:02:36,439 --> 00:02:40,159
a test that I would call absolutely brilliant.

50
00:02:40,159 --> 00:02:44,040
So, how do we get around this huge obstacle of missing data?

51
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:47,080
The authors had a brilliant analytical insight.

52
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:50,600
Instead of looking at the sentences handed down in trial courts,

53
00:02:50,599 --> 00:02:53,799
they focused directly on judicial errors.

54
00:02:53,799 --> 00:02:56,479
Specifically, they studied the death sentences

55
00:02:56,479 --> 00:02:58,919
that were overturned by higher courts.

56
00:02:58,919 --> 00:03:02,919
In the United States, for capital cases, there are two stages of automatic appeal.

57
00:03:02,919 --> 00:03:05,560
Direct appeal, handled by state courts,

58
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:08,439
and habeas corpus, handled by federal courts.

59
00:03:08,439 --> 00:03:11,159
The key idea here is to assume that higher courts

60
00:03:11,159 --> 00:03:15,439
intervene to correct the flaws and biases of the trial courts.

61
00:03:15,439 --> 00:03:16,079
Why?

62
00:03:16,079 --> 00:03:20,079
Because federal judges, for example, are further removed from local political pressures

63
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:23,960
and can evaluate the facts in a more detached and impartial way.

64
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:27,280
We come to the third point, the logic of the model,

65
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:30,040
how courts actually make decisions.

66
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:35,000
Theoretically, a trial court is always balancing between two great fears,

67
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,680
convicting an innocent person or letting a guilty person go free.

68
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:41,200
If we look at the original graphs of the paper,

69
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:45,480
we see exactly the seesaw effect of this highly delicate dynamic.

70
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:48,720
Let's imagine that a jury, perhaps even at an unconscious level,

71
00:03:48,759 --> 00:03:52,560
gives less weight to the extremely serious risk of convicting an innocent person

72
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:55,360
when the victim has certain characteristics.

73
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:59,840
Consequently, it will lower the threshold of certainty required to convict.

74
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:04,120
Mathematically, this increases the so-called Type I error.

75
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:07,879
In plain terms, more shaky convictions will be handed down

76
00:04:07,879 --> 00:04:13,199
and, naturally, there will be more errors that the higher courts will then have to overturn.

77
00:04:13,199 --> 00:04:18,160
And this is where the methodology of this study makes an incredible leap in quality.

78
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:22,040
Making comparisons relying solely and exclusively on the defendant's ethnicity

79
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:23,840
is deeply misleading,

80
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:27,840
because those hidden variables would continue to contaminate the data.

81
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:31,640
The genius lies in having used a rank-based test.

82
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:35,480
By keeping the defendant's ethnicity constant and changing only that of the victim,

83
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:38,520
they manage to surgically isolate the bias.

84
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:41,480
If we hold constant the fact that the defendant belongs to a minority

85
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:45,360
and we notice that the error rate changes drastically depending on whether the victim

86
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:46,920
is white or non-white,

87
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:50,280
boom, we have eliminated the other statistical excuses,

88
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:55,480
we are testing the court's behavior by changing just a single external factor.

89
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:59,280
Point number 4, the results on a national scale.

90
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:01,840
Let's go discover the actual data.

91
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:04,720
And, mind you, we are not talking about pure theory.

92
00:05:04,720 --> 00:05:09,240
These results derive from huge real databases across the United States,

93
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:13,280
between 1973 and 1995.

94
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:18,000
In the final stage of Habeas Corpus, when a minority defendant

95
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
kills a white victim, the rate of overturned sentences is 37.6%.

96
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,800
But if the victim is non-white, this rate plummets to 28.4%.

97
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:32,760
We are talking about a huge difference of a full 9 percentage points.

98
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:37,160
Think about the real weight of these numbers, we are talking about 9 percentage points

99
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:41,680
more of wrongful convictions, in trials where a person's life is at stake.

100
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:46,240
It is a massive and statistically robust systemic disparity.

101
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:49,959
Now, to maintain a totally impartial and scientific approach,

102
00:05:49,959 --> 00:05:51,920
we must turn the mirror around.

103
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:54,480
What happens with white defendants?

104
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:57,560
In these cases, the difference in error goes in the opposite direction,

105
00:05:57,560 --> 00:06:01,120
but the data clearly tell us that it remains statistically relevant.

106
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:04,920
There is no solid mathematical evidence of a reverse bias.

107
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:08,360
The truly strong distortion certified by the numbers

108
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:11,800
occurs only in that first and specific interracial combination.

109
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:14,439
Fifth point, the role of the South,

110
00:06:14,439 --> 00:06:18,360
a true geographical turning point in our investigation.

111
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:21,360
Yes, because that national model of bias

112
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:24,199
hides a crazy anomaly within it

113
00:06:24,199 --> 00:06:28,400
and is driven almost entirely by one specific American region, the South.

114
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:31,720
Listen to this number, 15.6.

115
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:34,520
15.6 percentage points.

116
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:38,359
This is the massive difference found in the American South.

117
00:06:38,359 --> 00:06:40,159
During the Habeas Corpus stage,

118
00:06:40,159 --> 00:06:44,359
for minority defendants with white victims, compared to non-white victims.

119
00:06:44,359 --> 00:06:48,319
A geographical disparity so large that it distorts the entire national average,

120
00:06:48,319 --> 00:06:52,039
showing how intense the phenomenon is in this specific area.

121
00:06:52,039 --> 00:06:54,240
And it becomes even more evident,

122
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:56,719
if we look at the first line of defense.

123
00:06:56,719 --> 00:07:00,759
Already in the very first stage of state direct appeal, still in the South,

124
00:07:00,759 --> 00:07:04,680
there is an initial difference of 3.3 percentage points.

125
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:09,839
A percentage that is then added to that 15.6% of the federal stage,

126
00:07:09,839 --> 00:07:13,719
all of this describes a real chain of systemic errors.

127
00:07:13,719 --> 00:07:17,920
With federal courts forced to continuously correct on multiple levels

128
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:20,599
the distortions coming from the South.

129
00:07:20,599 --> 00:07:24,039
Sixth and last point, the conclusions of the study.

130
00:07:24,039 --> 00:07:26,319
It's time to dismiss the alternatives.

131
00:07:26,319 --> 00:07:30,000
At this point, or scientifically speaking you might ask yourselves,

132
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:34,399
couldn't it be that these crimes were simply more serious than the others?

133
00:07:34,399 --> 00:07:35,600
Excellent question.

134
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:39,680
To answer, the authors applied so-called balancing tests,

135
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:41,879
highly rigorous mathematical tests,

136
00:07:41,879 --> 00:07:45,240
to verify if the crimes committed in these racial combinations

137
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:47,879
were more heinous or involved worse criminal records.

138
00:07:47,879 --> 00:07:52,399
The results show that characteristics are distributed in a perfectly homogeneous way,

139
00:07:52,399 --> 00:07:55,160
the crimes were nearly identical in severity.

140
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:57,000
Every factor was balanced,

141
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,040
leaving ethnicity as the only incontrovertible difference.

142
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:05,079
All other statistical explanations have been swept off the table.

143
00:08:05,079 --> 00:08:08,120
Summing up this exceptional work,

144
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:11,040
the cold numbers indicate to us without half measures

145
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:15,720
the existence of a clear racial bias within the justice system,

146
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:18,519
already rooted in the very first level of judgment.

147
00:08:18,519 --> 00:08:20,800
A bias that acts specifically,

148
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:21,920
according to the data,

149
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:25,560
against minority defendants who kill white victims,

150
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:29,959
and which emerges with impressive consistency precisely in the Southern states.

151
00:08:29,959 --> 00:08:34,480
Before we say goodbye, I leave you with this final, profound reflection.

152
00:08:34,480 --> 00:08:40,720
We know that, on national average, it takes a full nine years on death row

153
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:44,480
before an error is corrected through these appeals.

154
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:48,799
So, what is the true human and social cost of this bias?

155
00:08:48,799 --> 00:08:52,480
It is not just a graph on a rigorous economics paper,

156
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:54,200
it is a measurable reality,

157
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:57,920
that raises enormous questions about the fairness of justice

158
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:01,840
and the real impact of numbers on people's lives.

159
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:06,360
Let's always continue to explore, dig, and ask ourselves the right questions.

160
00:09:06,360 --> 00:09:09,560
Thank you for following this analysis. See you next time.